PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Core tension persists: explicit heterogeneity (distinct Y(1),Y(0)) allows valid instruments to identify different LATEs, so any overID statistic (Hansen J) can reject in population purely from heterogeneity rather than exclusion/exogeneity violations. This falsifies the “rejection guarantees invalidity” claim for J. AR tests a structural parameter (weak-IV robust) but not validity; plain t-test has none. 

Round-1 split exactly tracks prior: E camp (1,10-12,15,18-20,3,4,6,8) treats heterogeneity as decisive, ruling out A-C. B camp (13,14,16,17,2,5,7,9) claims the known-valid instrument supplies an “anchor” that absorbs LATE differences and restores J’s interpretation; this claim is unpersuasive because the question’s wording requires rejection to guarantee a violation even under heterogeneity, and the anchor logic still presupposes a common parameter that heterogeneity violates. 

No listed procedure survives the exact requirement. Persistent notes: problem supplies no extra assumptions (monotonicity + common support, constant effects, forward/reverse benchmark) that could rescue any option; unresolved whether question intends homogeneous subcase despite explicit wording. Answer remains E.}

